# Project Decomposition

This is too large to build/design as one system. It splits into four distinct pieces. Three are still unspecced; the fourth (Paper-Writing Pipeline) has a full design — see [`paper_writing_pipeline_design.md`](paper_writing_pipeline_design.md).

Shared architectural principle across all four: **no static, pre-built knowledge base and no fixed algorithm/tool catalog.** Agents reason live by searching the web (papers, code, benchmarks, venue guidelines) rather than looking things up in a maintained database. Any caching that happens (e.g. a venue template once found) grows organically as a side effect of use, not as an upfront data-engineering project.

## A. Live Retrieval & Tools Layer (foundational, shared — not a standalone spec)

Not a product on its own. A handful of live-lookup capabilities that B, C, and D call on demand:

- Web search / paper search (e.g. Semantic Scholar, arXiv, IEEE Xplore) for existing literature on a topic.
- Web search for "what's already been implemented" (code, benchmarks, existing algorithms) so agents can reason about what's novel vs already done, instead of consulting a fixed algorithm catalog.
- Venue guideline lookup (submission requirements, formatting rules) for a named conference/journal.

Each of these gets built as a tool alongside whichever of B/C/D needs it first, rather than as its own upfront project.

## B. Discovery & Recommendation Agent (not yet designed)

The scholar's first-touch entry point.

- Scholar selects a research domain and problem area.
- Agent **brainstorms novel/undiscovered topics itself** by researching the web live — reading what's been published and implemented in the domain to find gaps — rather than pulling from a static topic/algorithm catalog.
- Suggests a research approach: tools, novel algorithms, training methods, datasets — reasoned live from what it finds, not looked up from a fixed list.
- Recommends relevant IEEE papers to reference.

Depends on the Live Retrieval layer (A). Not yet brainstormed in detail — next sub-project to design.

## C. Evaluation & Grading Agent (not yet designed)

The "is this publishable" checkpoint, sitting between doing the research and writing it up.

- Input: uploaded research results — files, graphs, plots, results, references.
- Evaluates novelty (by searching live for similar/prior work), research depth, and overall publishability.
- Grades out of 10 across specific criteria: novelty, rigor, completeness.
- **Decision — Publishable?**
  - **No** → suggests concrete improvements (more experiments, seed increase, further research, stronger baselines) and loops the scholar back to "conduct more research."
  - **Yes** → scholar can proceed to the Paper-Writing Pipeline (D), if they want a paper generated.

Depends on the Live Retrieval layer (A), to check novelty against existing live literature. Not yet brainstormed in detail.

## D. Paper-Writing Pipeline (fully designed)

Given research artifacts (code, plots, results, notes, reference papers) already evaluated as publishable, plus a target venue, produces a submission-shaped LaTeX paper — properly structured, no blank/broken layout, naturally written, internally consistent.

Built as an MCP server; Claude (in Claude Code or Claude Desktop, using the user's own subscription) is the orchestrating agent across four stages — Screening, Structure/Drafting, Humanization, Final QA — with feedback loops routing issues back to the responsible stage.

Full design, architecture, tool list, error handling, and testing strategy: [`paper_writing_pipeline_design.md`](paper_writing_pipeline_design.md).
